     1 +Description: Preserve input-method serial ordering across keyboard-grab keys
     2 +
     3 +Sway currently forwards unhandled key events to an input method through
     4 +zwp_input_method_keyboard_grab_v2.key and continues processing text-input
     5 +commits independently. A text-input commit causes Sway to send
     6 +zwp_input_method_v2.done, and wlroots increments the input-method serial for
     7 +each done. However, the keyboard-grab key event has no protocol-level response
     8 +or acknowledgement serial.
     9 +
    10 +This creates a real ordering bug:
    11 +
    12 +  1. Sway forwards key K to the input method.
    13 +  2. Before the input method replies to K, the focused text-input client
    14 +     commits updated state, so Sway sends a new input-method done.
    17 +  4. wlroots rejects that commit as stale because Sway has already advanced the
    18 +     serial with the intervening done.
    19 +
    20 +That loses the committed text. This is visible with fast typing because several
    21 +key events and text-input state commits can be in flight before IBus has
    22 +finished processing the earlier key through D-Bus.
    23 +
    24 +Fix this by making Sway preserve the ordering between input-method keyboard
    25 +grab keys and input-method done events. Forwarded key presses create an
    26 +outstanding response count. Text-input state updates are snapshotted and queued
    27 +behind outstanding key presses. If another key arrives while state is queued,
    28 +the key is queued behind that state, so sequences such as K1, S1, K2, S2 are
    29 +delivered to the input method as K1, then S1, then K2, then S2. A key response
    30 +is considered observed when Sway receives either an input_method.commit or a
    31 +same-client virtual-keyboard key press, which is the observable path used when
    32 +the input method forwards an unhandled key instead of committing text.
    33 +
    34 +This is intentionally a serial-ordering fix in the compositor relay. It does
    35 +not paper over the stale commit in wlroots: wlroots is following the protocol
    36 +when it ignores a commit whose serial does not match the number of done events
    37 +already issued.
    38 +

Done. Patch is here: fix-sway-im-serial-key-barrier.patch.

  I also built it through Guix:

  guix build --with-patch=sway=/home/dannym/src/auto/e/ibus-loss/fix-sway-im-serial-key-barrier.patch sway

  It succeeded and produced:

  /gnu/store/42z8jvrffrscjpav3b9as0rp17l4f59q-sway-1.11

  The actual bug: Sway was letting zwp_input_method_v2.done advance the IM serial while a key already forwarded through zwp_input_method_keyboard_grab_v2.key
  could still answer with a commit using the older serial. wlroots then rejects that commit per protocol. The patch makes Sway preserve ordering: forwarded key
  presses create an outstanding response count; text-input state/done snapshots wait behind those keys; later keys wait behind queued state.

  Symbols: K = forwarded key press, R = observable IM response, S = text-input state update/done, V = same-client virtual-keyboard forwarded key response, G =
  grab destroyed, I = IM destroyed.

  | # | Order | Patched behavior |
  |---|---|---|
  | 1 | S | send D(S) immediately. OK. |
  | 2 | K R | key sent, response accepted at same serial. OK. |
  | 3 | K S R | queue S; R; send D(S). OK, fixes stale commit. |
  | 4 | K R S | R clears key; S immediate. OK. |
  | 5 | K S S R | queue both states; R; send D(S1), D(S2). OK. |
  | 6 | K S R S | first S flushes after R; second immediate. OK. |
  | 7 | K K R R | both keys use same state; both responses accepted. OK. |
  | 8 | K K S R R | S waits until both responses. OK. |
  | 9 | K K S R2 R1 | response order does not matter; S waits for count zero. OK. |
  | 10 | K S K R R | second K queued; after R1: D(S), send K2; R2. OK. |
  | 11 | K S K S R R | S1,K2,S2 queued; flushes in that order. OK. |
  | 12 | K S K K R R R | after R1: D(S), send K2,K3; wait for both. OK. |
  | 13 | K K S K R R R | K3 waits behind S; S waits behind K1,K2. OK. |
  | 14 | K S K R S R | S2 queues behind K2. OK. |
  | 15 | K S K R R S | after queued key resolves, later S immediate. OK. |
  | 16 | S K R | state serial advances before key; key response uses new serial. OK. |
  | 17 | S K S R | second state waits behind key. OK. |
  | 18 | S K S K R R | K2 waits behind S2; ordered. OK. |
  | 19 | S S K R | both states sent before key. OK. |
  | 20 | K S V | forwarded-key response V clears key; then D(S). OK. |
  | 21 | K S K V R | V1 flushes S, sends K2; R2 accepted. OK. |
  | 22 | K K S V R | S waits for both observable responses. OK. |
  | 23 | K S V S | V flushes first state; second state immediate. OK. |
  | 24 | K S K V S R | S1 before K2; S2 waits for K2. OK. |
  | 25 | K S G | pending key abandoned with grab; queued state flushes. OK. |
  | 26 | K S K G | queued key dropped because grab is gone; state flushes. OK. |
  | 27 | K S I | queue cleared because IM is gone. OK, no stale commit possible. |
  | 28 | K S with no R | serial-safe but waits; protocol has no ack to prove progress. |
  | 29 | K S K with no R | queued state/key wait; avoids loss, but IM silence stalls. |
  | 30 | K S R(stale old world) | with patch, Sway did not send D(S) before R, so it is not stale. OK. |

  I added source comments explaining exactly why the barrier exists, including the missing keyboard-grab ack and the IM commit serial rule.

